.TH "VIDEOWMARK" "1" "2026\-10\-16" "videowmark" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
videowmark \(em video watermarking tool (using audio track)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]videowmark\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] \f[I]command\f[R]
[\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]videowmark\f[R] creates watermarked video files containing an
embedded message, and can retrieve an embedded message from a previously
watermarked video.
.PP
Messages are provided as hexadecimal data and embedded in the audio
track of the video, intended to be robust against common audio
processing operations.
.PP
\f[B]videowmark\f[R] is a native program.
It needs no shell, Bash or Python at run time.
It runs \f[B]ffmpeg\f[R] and \f[B]ffprobe\f[R] to read and write media,
and \f[B]audiowmark\f[R] to embed and detect the watermark (see
\f[I]TOOLS\f[R]).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]add\f[R] \f[I]input_video\f[R] \f[I]watermarked_video\f[R] \f[I]message_hex\f[R]
Create a watermarked video file containing the provided message.
The video stream is copied without recompression.
The audio stream is watermarked and encoded again with the same codec
(see \f[I]AUDIO CODECS\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]get\f[R] \f[I]watermarked_video\f[R]
Retrieve the embedded message from a watermarked video.
The report of \f[B]audiowmark get\f[R] is printed to standard output:
one \f[I]pattern\f[R] line per detected message, with its time position,
the detected hexadecimal message and detection values.
.TP
\f[B]probe\f[R] \f[I]input_video\f[R]
Print the input path followed by a short codec and bit rate summary in
encoder\-option style, for example \f[CR]\-c:a mp3 \-ab 128000\f[R].
This is the legacy summary kept from earlier releases.
It is not the exact encoder selection that \f[B]add\f[R] makes; for
example, \f[B]add\f[R] encodes MP3 with \f[B]libmp3lame\f[R] and Vorbis
with \f[B]libvorbis\f[R].
Only stream information is read; no media file is written.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options apply to all commands.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-strength\f[R] \f[I]s\f[R]
Set watermark strength.
The value is passed to \f[B]audiowmark\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-key\f[R] \f[I]file\f[R]
Load watermarking key material from \f[I]file\f[R].
The value is passed to \f[B]audiowmark\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-q\f[R], \f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Disable informational messages.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Enable verbose output from the underlying \f[B]ffmpeg\f[R] processing.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\f[I]input_video\f[R]
Path to the input (unwatermarked) video file.
It must contain exactly one audio stream and exactly one video stream.
.TP
\f[I]watermarked_video\f[R]
Path to the output (watermarked) video file (for \f[B]add\f[R]), or the
input watermarked video file (for \f[B]get\f[R]).
.TP
\f[I]message_hex\f[R]
Message to embed, provided as hexadecimal digits.
The default watermark payload is 128 bits.
Use 32 hexadecimal digits for the full 128\-bit payload, for example
\f[CR]0123456789abcdef0011223344556677\f[R].
A shorter message is repeated to fill the payload, for compatibility
with earlier releases.
Messages longer than 32 hexadecimal digits are rejected.
.SH AUDIO CODECS
\f[B]add\f[R] keeps the codec of the input audio stream.
The supported codecs and the encoders they are written with are:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
